embassy-stm32: enable a timer channel's main and complementary outputs independently

Answers embassy-rs/embassy#3710. ComplementaryPwm::enable() and disable() switch
CCxE and CCxNE together, so a channel's main output cannot be driven while its
complementary output stays off. Four methods are added beside them and nothing
existing changes: set_main_output_enable, set_complementary_output_enable and the
two matching getters. The complementary getter needs a read-back primitive that
timer/low_level.rs did not have (it has one for CCxE only), so one is added there
next to enable_complementary_channel.

The names are this patch's choice; the ones sketched in the issue are not an
agreed API. "main"/"complementary" is the vocabulary the same file already uses
for set_main_polarity / set_complementary_polarity and for
set_normal_output_idle_state / set_complementary_output_idle_state.

Against embassy revision 27d398592b8a8c415c54eb04a0893aa9ef20316d. An upstream
pull request would add a CHANGELOG entry as well; that file is not part of the
offline reference copy, so this patch leaves it alone. Both hunks carry three
lines of trailing context: `git apply` requires a hunk whose context is all
leading to match at the end of the file, and both of these insert in the middle
of one.

diff --git a/embassy-stm32/src/timer/complementary_pwm.rs b/embassy-stm32/src/timer/complementary_pwm.rs
--- a/embassy-stm32/src/timer/complementary_pwm.rs
+++ b/embassy-stm32/src/timer/complementary_pwm.rs
@@ -499,7 +499,42 @@
     pub fn disable(&mut self, channel: Channel) {
         self.inner.enable_complementary_channel(channel, false);
         self.inner.enable_channel(channel, false);
     }
+
+    /// Enable or disable the main output (`CCxE`) of the given channel on its own.
+    ///
+    /// The complementary output (`CCxNE`) is left exactly as it is, so a channel's
+    /// two outputs can be switched independently. While only one of the two is
+    /// enabled the hardware drives it straight from `OCxREF`, with no dead time
+    /// inserted, and the complementary output on its own is not inverted; the
+    /// output that is disabled is released by the timer, so its pin floats unless
+    /// `OSSR`/`OSSI` select an off-state level. Dead time is inserted again as soon
+    /// as both outputs of the channel are enabled.
+    ///
+    /// [`enable`](Self::enable) and [`disable`](Self::disable) keep switching both
+    /// outputs of a channel together.
+    pub fn set_main_output_enable(&mut self, channel: Channel, enable: bool) {
+        self.inner.enable_channel(channel, enable);
+    }
+
+    /// Enable or disable the complementary output (`CCxNE`) of the given channel on its own.
+    ///
+    /// The main output (`CCxE`) is left exactly as it is. See
+    /// [`set_main_output_enable`](Self::set_main_output_enable) for what the
+    /// hardware drives while only one output of a channel is enabled.
+    pub fn set_complementary_output_enable(&mut self, channel: Channel, enable: bool) {
+        self.inner.enable_complementary_channel(channel, enable);
+    }
+
+    /// Whether the main output (`CCxE`) of the given channel is enabled.
+    pub fn get_main_output_enable(&self, channel: Channel) -> bool {
+        self.inner.get_channel_enable_state(channel)
+    }
+
+    /// Whether the complementary output (`CCxNE`) of the given channel is enabled.
+    pub fn get_complementary_output_enable(&self, channel: Channel) -> bool {
+        self.inner.get_complementary_channel_enable_state(channel)
+    }

     /// Set PWM frequency.
     ///
diff --git a/embassy-stm32/src/timer/low_level.rs b/embassy-stm32/src/timer/low_level.rs
--- a/embassy-stm32/src/timer/low_level.rs
+++ b/embassy-stm32/src/timer/low_level.rs
@@ -1613,7 +1613,12 @@
         self.regs_advanced()
             .ccer()
             .modify(|w| w.set_ccne(channel.index(), enable));
     }
+
+    /// Get enable/disable state of a complementary channel.
+    pub fn get_complementary_channel_enable_state(&self, channel: Channel) -> bool {
+        self.regs_advanced().ccer().read().ccne(channel.index())
+    }

